# what is this pattern and what we are acheiving with it


The architectural pattern used in the code is called the Transactional Outbox Pattern.
In a modern microservices or event-driven system, a single business action often requires doing two things:

   1. Updating your local database (e.g., changing a workflow status from DRAFT to PENDING).
   2. Notifying other services (e.g., sending an event to [Apache Kafka](https://kafka.apache.org/), an SNS topic, or a notification service).

------------------------------
## The Problem This Pattern Solves (The Dual-Write Problem)
If you try to update your database and publish an event to Kafka back-to-back in your standard application code, one of them will eventually fail.

* Scenario A: You update the database first, but Kafka is down or a network blip occurs. The database saves the change, but the rest of your system never finds out. Your data is now inconsistent.
* Scenario B: You publish to Kafka first, but the database update fails and rolls back. Now your system is broadcasting an event for data that technically doesn't exist.

------------------------------
## How the Outbox Pattern Works
Instead of trying to talk to the database and an external message broker at the same time, you force them into a single transaction:

   1. The Single Database Transaction: When a user clicks "Submit", your application opens a single database transaction. It writes the status change to your workflows table and inserts a raw event record into a dedicated outbox table inside the same database.
   2. Atomicity Guaranteed: Because relational databases support ACID transactions, either both writes succeed, or both fail. There is zero risk of an inconsistent state.
   3. The Relay Process: A separate background process (often called an Outbox Relay, Change Data Capture (CDC) engine, or Debezium) constantly polls the outbox table. When it sees a new row, it reads it, publishes it to your message broker (like Kafka), and then deletes or marks the outbox row as processed.

------------------------------
## What We Are Achieving With It

* At-Least-Once Delivery Guarantees: You gain a 100% guarantee that every single status change in your application will eventually be broadcasted to the rest of your system. Messages cannot be dropped due to network blips.
* High Performance (Asynchronous Decoupling): Your web API doesn't have to wait for Kafka or an email service to respond before returning a success message to the user. It only waits for a fast local database write.
* Data Resiliency: If Kafka or downstream consumer services crash for 4 hours, your application keeps working perfectly. The events simply queue up safely in your database outbox table and get processed automatically once everything recovers.



